ZkPassportMethodVerifyTest
├── when all six checks pass
│   └── it should return the verify selector
├── when the proof reaches the root verifier
│   └── it should call it once with the accepted version and a service config ending in false
├── when the proof reaches the verifier helper
│   └── it should pass the proofs committed inputs to get bound data
├── when the configured identifier is zero
│   ├── it should refuse the proof
│   └── it should call no verifier
├── when the root accepts the proof only as a development proof
│   └── it should refuse the proof
├── when the root answers valid false
│   ├── it should refuse the proof
│   └── it should not call the verifier helper
├── when the root verifier fails
│   └── it should refuse the proof
├── when the root verifier returns nothing
│   └── it should refuse the proof
├── when the root verifier returns one word
│   └── it should refuse the proof
├── when the root verifier returns oversized garbage
│   └── it should refuse the proof
├── when the root verifier word holds no code
│   └── it should refuse the proof
├── when the returned identifier is zero
│   ├── it should refuse the proof
│   └── it should not call the verifier helper
├── when the returned identifier differs from the config
│   ├── it should refuse the proof
│   └── it should not call the verifier helper
├── when the returned identifier differs only in its high bits
│   └── it should refuse the proof
├── when the returned verifier helper is not the stored verifier helper
│   ├── it should refuse the proof
│   └── it should not call the returned verifier helper
├── when the returned verifier helper is the zero address
│   └── it should refuse the proof
├── when the nullifier type input is not zero
│   ├── it should refuse the proof
│   ├── it should call the root verifier once
│   └── it should not call the verifier helper
├── when the nullifier type is none and the identifier matches
│   └── it should refuse the proof
├── when the nullifier type input is nonzero only in its high bits
│   └── it should refuse the proof
├── when only another input is zero
│   └── it should refuse the proof
├── when there are exactly three public inputs and the first is zero
│   └── it should accept the proof
├── when there are two public inputs
│   ├── it should refuse the proof
│   └── it should not call the verifier helper
├── when custom data is the uppercase rendering
│   └── it should refuse the proof
├── when custom data is the unprefixed rendering
│   └── it should refuse the proof
├── when custom data carries an uppercase x in the prefix
│   └── it should refuse the proof
├── when custom data carries a trailing character
│   └── it should refuse the proof
├── when custom data is empty
│   └── it should refuse the proof
├── when custom data is the raw digest bytes
│   └── it should refuse the proof
├── when the digest has leading zeros
│   └── it should accept the full width rendering
├── when custom data is the short rendering of a digest with leading zeros
│   └── it should refuse the proof
├── when the digest is zero
│   └── it should accept the rendering with sixty four zeros
├── when the verifier helper fails
│   └── it should refuse the proof
├── when the verifier helper returns nothing
│   └── it should refuse the proof
├── when the verifier helper returns one word
│   └── it should refuse the proof
├── when the verifier helper returns oversized garbage
│   └── it should refuse the proof
├── when the proof is empty
│   └── it should refuse the proof
├── when the config is empty
│   └── it should refuse the proof
├── when the method is stopped and lifted
│   └── it should give the same answer before, during and after the stop
├── when a failing proof arrives during a stop
│   └── it should refuse the proof
├── when the keys name a new root and verifier helper
│   ├── it should call the new root and not the old one
│   └── it should accept the proof
├── when the keys name another verifier helper
│   └── it should refuse a root answer naming the former verifier helper
├── when the key admin moves the accepted version after a config is committed
│   ├── it should call the root verifier once with the new accepted version
│   └── it should accept the proof
├── when the root accepts the proof only under the former version
│   └── it should refuse the proof
├── when the method accepts any version and the config holds any values
│   ├── it should call the root verifier once with the accepted version
│   └── it should accept the proof
├── when a proof is verified
│   └── it should call none of the snapshot addresses
└── when the method is deployed with a zero guardian word
    └── it should accept an honest proof
